- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
 "research_concepts": [
  "Player role definition (shapers, cut-heavy, drive-heavy)",
  "Role transferability across contexts (seasons, teams)",
  "Offensive play types (cuts, drives)",
  "Player impact assessment"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "How to identify player roles based on play-type data?",
  "How to quantify the generalizability of a player's role effectiveness across different teams or seasons?",
  "What confounding factors need to be controlled when comparing player impact across different team contexts?",
  "How to measure the effectiveness of a player's role in relation to team success or efficiency?"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF-PAP-009",
   "status_as_recorded": "supported",
   "finding": "Lineup coverage (>=1 organizer, shaper, terminal) varies within team, is separable from talent/counts, and talent control behaves. G1 72 team-seasons with >=200 poss in both; G2 |rho(COVERED,TALENT)|=0.042, aux R2=0.203; G3 rho(TALENT,PPP)=+0.027. Gate passed."
  },
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-029",
   "status_as_recorded": "artifact",
   "finding": "Play-type actions have decaying cross-possession memory (lag-1 effect >= lag-2; not just score margin or team quality). FAIL decay-sanity: DRIVE lifts +0.015,+0.020,+0.018,+0.021,+0.020 (flat). Within-team: DRIVE lag-1 +0.015 to -0.002. Cohen d ~0.01-0.03; p~0 from n>150k. Callable play-type horizons = 0. Flat non-decaying lifts collapse under within-team baseline; team-quality confound."
  },
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF29",
   "status_as_recorded": "rejected",
   "finding": "Lineups covering all three core roles score more per possession net of role counts and talent. Final: beta=+0.42 pts/100, t=0.53, p=0.595, CI[-1.15,+1.90]; MDE80=2.19 pts/100; n_ORGANIZER +2.35 (t=4.62). Prereg-recorded run (2026-07-13): +0.67 pts/100, t=1.35, p=0.176, CI[-0.28,+1.65]. Null result."
  },
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-044",
   "status_as_recorded": "supported",
   "finding": "The oracle BEST player for a counter play type varies by opponent, not a single team identity. 64/78 (82%) (off, PT) cells have >1 oracle player across opponents; 19/78 dominated by one player (>=90%); median oracle-player entropy 0.928. 82% of cells vary the oracle player by opponent."
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-029",
   "what_failed": "Attempt to measure persistent impact of play-type actions (like DRIVE) using cross-possession memory.",
   "why": "C_artifact (apparent effect produced by a construction, selection or labelling flaw). Flat non-decaying lifts collapsed under within-team baseline, indicating a team-quality confound."
  },
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF29",
   "what_failed": "Hypothesis that lineups covering all three core roles score more per possession net of role counts and talent.",
   "why": "B_powered_null (adequately powered null result). The observed effect was not statistically significant with a large MDE."
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "Defining structural roles (like 'shaper') and measuring their coverage in lineups is feasible (FAM:SF-PAP-009).",
  "Assessing the impact of specific play-type actions (e.g., DRIVE) is highly susceptible to confounding by overall team quality, requiring robust within-team baselines or equivalent controls (FAM:SF-CHT-029).",
  "Player effectiveness for specific play types is often context-dependent, varying by opponent, suggesting that a player's 'best' contribution may not be uniformly transferable (FAM:SF-APPA-044).",
  "Detecting a significant, independent impact of role coverage on team success can be challenging, even with talent controls, and may require substantial statistical power (FAM:SF29)."
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "A robust, project-standard definition for 'cut-heavy shaper' or 'drive-heavy shaper'.",
  "Specific methods to adequately control for team quality, opponent effects, and teammate skillsets when assessing *individual player role transferability* across different contexts (e.g., seasons, teams).",
  "Whether the available data provides sufficient sample size and statistical power to differentiate the transferability of these specific role types, especially given the potential rarity of truly 'heavy' archetypes for individual players and the need for cross-team/season observations."
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "The research question requires a clear definition of 'shaper' and quantitative metrics for 'cut-heavy' and 'drive-heavy' based on play-type initiation data.",
  "Measuring 'travel better' implies comparing a player's impact or efficiency in their defined role across different team contexts (e.g., pre/post-trade, different seasons with different teams).",
  "Given the confounding risks identified in historical evidence, robust controls for team quality, talent, and potentially opponent/teammate context are essential to isolate the effect of role transferability."
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "The precise definition of 'shaper' within the project's framework, and how 'cut-heavy' vs 'drive-heavy' will be quantified.",
  "The specific metric to be used for 'role effectiveness' or 'impact' (e.g., PPP, adjusted +/-).",
  "The number of players who consistently fit the 'cut-heavy shaper' or 'drive-heavy shaper' definition across multiple team/season contexts, which will dictate the effective sample size for comparison."
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes",
  "explanation": "The historical evidence, particularly FAM:SF-CHT-029, highlighted a critical confounding risk (team quality) for assessing play-type impact that would directly undermine the proposed research question if not addressed. FAM:SF-APPA-044 further emphasized the context-dependency of player effectiveness. These findings shift the decision from a straightforward 'proceed' to 'proceed_with_changes' by mandating specific methodological adjustments to control for confounding factors and acknowledging the complexity of isolating role transferability. The evidence from FAM:SF29 also raises concerns about power for detecting subtle effects of roles."
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": 0.6,
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "CONFOUNDING",
    "text": "Strong confounding by team quality, player talent, teammate characteristics, and opponent specificities could obscure the true effect of role transferability if not rigorously controlled."
   },
   {
    "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
    "text": "The number of players who fit specific 'cut-heavy' or 'drive-heavy' shaper archetypes and also change teams/contexts frequently enough to assess 'travel' might be small, leading to low statistical power."
   },
   {
    "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
    "text": "Reliably defining 'cut-heavy' and 'drive-heavy' shapers and their 'effectiveness' in a way that is consistent across different team schemes and player usages is challenging."
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "no",
  "cited": [
   "FAM:SF-PAP-009",
   "FAM:SF-CHT-029",
   "FAM:SF29",
   "FAM:SF-APPA-044"
  ],
  "summary": "Previous work established the feasibility of defining structural roles and measuring their coverage (FAM:SF-PAP-009) but found no significant impact of lineup role coverage (FAM:SF29). Critically, it identified significant confounding by team quality when assessing play-type impact (FAM:SF-CHT-029) and demonstrated opponent-specificity of player effectiveness (FAM:SF-APPA-044). While these studies touch on role definition and impact, none directly compare the transferability of 'cut-heavy' vs 'drive-heavy' shapers."
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "CONFOUNDING",
   "text": "The primary risk is confounding by team quality, overall player talent, specific teammate skillsets (e.g., spacing for drivers vs. cutters), and opponent defensive schemes. A player's role might appear to 'travel better' due to moving to a better team, having more complementary teammates, or facing weaker defenses, rather than the intrinsic transferability of their role."
  },
  {
   "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
   "text": "The number of players who consistently operate as either 'cut-heavy' or 'drive-heavy' shapers and change teams or have significant role changes across seasons to enable a 'travel' assessment might be insufficient for robust statistical comparison, particularly when controlling for multiple confounders. The available N=24 units (team-seasons) might be too small for individual player analysis."
  },
  {
   "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
   "text": "Defining 'cut-heavy' and 'drive-heavy' shapers objectively and reliably from play-type data, and consistently measuring their 'effectiveness' across diverse team systems, presents a challenge. The chosen metrics must accurately reflect the intended role and its impact."
  },
  {
   "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
   "text": "Individual players might not have sufficient exposure in both 'cut-heavy' and 'drive-heavy' contexts (or across different teams/seasons) to reliably estimate their role's transferability, especially if their play style is very specialized."
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": 0.2,
  "rejected": 0.4,
  "untestable": 0.2,
  "artifact": 0.1,
  "uncertain": 0.1
 },
 "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "The difference in the change in player role-adjusted offensive efficiency (e.g., PPP over expected) when a player's team context changes, for 'cut-heavy shapers' versus 'drive-heavy shapers'. Specifically, we will compare the average absolute deviation from a player's baseline role efficiency when moving to a new team/season, expecting 'cut-heavy' shapers to show smaller deviations.",
  "unit": "Player-season-team observations. A 'player-context-change' unit would be defined as a player moving from Team A (Season X) to Team B (Season Y), or a significant role change within the same team across seasons.",
  "sample": "All players identified as 'shapers' (initiating >X% of possessions for their team) in the 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25, and 2025-26 NBA regular seasons and playoffs. Players must have at least two distinct team-season observations with sufficient minutes/possessions (e.g., >500 possessions) to be included. Players will be categorized as 'cut-heavy' or 'drive-heavy' based on the ratio of initiated cut play types to initiated drive play types within their shaper possessions (e.g., top/bottom quartile of this ratio among shapers).",
  "power": "A minimum of 50 player-context-change units per group (cut-heavy, drive-heavy) is desired. Power calculation will be performed assuming a medium effect size (Cohen's d=0.5) for the difference in role efficiency deviation, aiming for 80% power at alpha=0.05. If the required sample size is not met, the study will be flagged as underpowered.",
  "controls": "Multivariate regression model controlling for: 1) Player talent (e.g., previous season's adjusted offensive rating, career box-score metrics), 2) Teammate quality (e.g., average offensive rating of teammates on court with the player, excluding the player), 3) Team offensive scheme (e.g., team-level play-type frequencies), 4) Opponent strength (e.g., average defensive rating of opponents faced). A player-fixed effects model will be considered to control for unobserved player-specific characteristics. Within-player changes across contexts will be prioritized to mitigate team-quality confounds (as per FAM:SF-CHT-029).",
  "falsification": "The null hypothesis is that there is no significant difference in role transferability (measured by deviation in efficiency) between cut-heavy and drive-heavy shapers. A significant difference would be required to support the research question. A 'sanity check' will involve examining whether the chosen efficiency metric correlates with team win-loss records or playoff success, and whether the 'shaper' definitions align with expert basketball knowledge.",
  "multiplicity": "No multiple testing correction will be applied for the primary hypothesis test. If secondary analyses are performed (e.g., examining specific types of cuts/drives), appropriate corrections (e.g., Bonferroni, FDR) will be considered.",
  "decision_rule": "The research question will be considered 'supported' if the p-value for the difference in role transferability between cut-heavy and drive-heavy shapers is < 0.05, and the observed effect is in the hypothesized direction, with adequate power. Otherwise, it will be 'rejected' (if powered) or 'uncertain' (if underpowered).",
  "data_version": "NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25, 2025-26 regular season and playoffs play-by-play, substitution rotation intervals, starters, defensive matchup minutes, box-score summary, and derived play-type possession records."
 }

